---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const canonicalUrl = 'https://handheldsettings.com/legion-go-settings/cyberpunk-2077/';
const title = 'Cyberpunk 2077 Legion Go settings: 800p/40 FPS baseline — Handheld Settings Lab';
const description = 'Tune Cyberpunk 2077 on the original Lenovo Legion Go with a cautious 800p/40 FPS baseline, Steam Deck preset, FSR guidance, benchmark testing, and 70GB SSD planning.';
const image = '/assets/games/cyberpunk-2077-header.jpg';

const sources = [
  {
    label: 'Steam: Cyberpunk 2077 store page, current PC requirements, controller support, and official media',
    url: 'https://store.steampowered.com/app/1091500/Cyberpunk_2077/'
  },
  {
    label: 'CD PROJEKT RED: official Cyberpunk 2077 site',
    url: 'https://www.cyberpunk.net/us/en/'
  },
  {
    label: 'Lenovo PSREF: original Legion Go 8APU1 specifications',
    url: 'https://psref.lenovo.com/Product/Legion/Legion_Go_8APU1'
  },
  {
    label: 'Lenovo Support: drivers and software lookup',
    url: 'https://pcsupport.lenovo.com/'
  }
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What Cyberpunk 2077 settings should I use on the original Legion Go?',
    answer: 'Start at 1280×800, the Steam Deck preset, FSR in Quality mode, ray tracing off, and a 40 FPS cap in a performance-class power profile. Run the built-in benchmark and then test a busy drive through Night City. This is a cautious editorial baseline, not a personally measured guarantee.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Legion Go run Cyberpunk 2077 at 60 FPS?',
    answer: 'A 60 FPS target may require more aggressive upscaling, lower settings, frame generation, or a higher power profile, and it may not remain stable in crowded areas. Begin at 40 FPS for a better clarity-and-consistency balance, then test 60 as a separate profile rather than assuming it will hold everywhere.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use 800p or 1200p for Cyberpunk 2077 on Legion Go?',
    answer: 'Use 800p for the first performance baseline. Test 1200p only after the same benchmark and busy route are stable, keeping the power mode and frame cap unchanged. The native 1600p panel does not mean this demanding game should render at 1600p.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should ray tracing be enabled on Legion Go?',
    answer: 'Leave ray tracing off for this baseline. It adds substantial rendering load and is a poor first trade on integrated graphics when the priority is consistent handheld play. Restore conventional lighting and reflection settings selectively instead.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How much storage does Cyberpunk 2077 need?',
    answer: 'Steam currently lists 70GB of available space and requires an SSD. Leave extra room for patches, shader data, saves, Windows temporary files, and optional content rather than filling the drive to exactly 70GB.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Were these Legion Go settings personally benchmarked?',
    answer: 'No. Handheld Settings Lab did not personally test the current game, driver, Windows, or Legion Space build. The profile is an official-spec and public configuration-pattern-informed starting point, so no frame rate, battery runtime, or thermal result is presented as a measurement.'
  }
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Cyberpunk 2077 Legion Go settings: 800p/40 FPS baseline',
    description,
    image: `https://handheldsettings.com${image}`,
    url: canonicalUrl,
    datePublished: '2026-07-28',
    dateModified: '2026-07-28',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Handheld Settings Lab' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Handheld Settings Lab', url: 'https://handheldsettings.com/' },
    about: ['Cyberpunk 2077', 'Lenovo Legion Go', 'game settings', 'frame rate', 'handheld PC']
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://handheldsettings.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Legion Go settings', item: 'https://handheldsettings.com/legion-go-settings/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Cyberpunk 2077 settings', item: canonicalUrl }
    ]
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map(faq => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: faq.question,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faq.answer }
    }))
  }
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} image={image} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="wrap article">
    <span class="eyebrow">Legion Go settings · Baseline published July 2026</span>
    <h1>Cyberpunk 2077 Legion Go settings: start at 800p and 40 FPS</h1>
    <p class="lede muted">Cyberpunk 2077 has a useful built-in benchmark, but Night City traffic, crowds, combat, and rapid traversal are the real test. This original Legion Go profile starts at 800p, the Steam Deck preset, and a 40 FPS cap before restoring clarity one setting at a time. It is official-spec and public configuration-pattern-informed guidance—not a personally tested benchmark.</p>

    <figure class="game-hero">
      <img src={image} alt="Official Cyberpunk 2077 Steam header artwork showing V against a yellow Night City background" width="460" height="215" />
      <figcaption class="media-credit">Official Cyberpunk 2077 Steam store header image. Source: <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1091500/Cyberpunk_2077/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">CD PROJEKT RED's Steam store page</a>. Artwork and trademarks belong to their owners; use does not imply endorsement.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="settings-panel">
      <section class="card"><h3>Starting target</h3><div class="stat">800p / 40</div><p class="muted">Baseline to test, not a guarantee</p></section>
      <section class="card"><h3>Starting preset</h3><p><strong>Steam Deck</strong></p><p class="muted">Then tune the largest costs</p></section>
      <section class="card"><h3>Upscaling</h3><p><strong>FSR Quality</strong></p><p class="muted">Balanced if the busy-route test fails</p></section>
      <section class="card"><h3>Storage floor</h3><div class="stat">70GB</div><p class="muted">Official requirement; SSD required</p></section>
    </div>

    <div class="callout"><strong>Do two tests:</strong> run the built-in benchmark and record its result, then drive the same busy city route and enter one repeatable combat encounter. A preset that looks stable only in the benchmark is not fully validated.</div>

    <h2>Recommended Cyberpunk 2077 Legion Go baseline</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Start here</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Legion Go power mode</td><td>Performance-class profile</td><td>Creates a repeatable starting condition. Record whether the device is on battery or a compatible charger.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Display refresh rate</td><td>60Hz for the baseline</td><td>Makes a 40 FPS cap easy to distinguish from an uncapped target; panel refresh does not create rendered frames.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>In-game resolution</td><td>1280×800</td><td>Preserves the panel’s 16:10 shape while greatly reducing render load compared with native 2560×1600.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frame-rate cap</td><td>40 FPS</td><td>Targets a practical balance between controller response, image quality, and demanding city scenes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quick preset</td><td>Steam Deck</td><td>Provides a conservative official in-game starting preset before individual changes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AMD FidelityFX Super Resolution</td><td>Quality</td><td>Starts with the clearer upscaling mode. Move to Balanced only when the repeatable route needs more headroom.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dynamic resolution scaling</td><td>Off for first comparison</td><td>Keeps image resolution consistent while comparing settings. Test dynamic scaling later if occasional drops matter more than changing sharpness.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Texture quality</td><td>Medium</td><td>Retains useful surface detail without making the first profile overly aggressive on shared memory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local shadow quality</td><td>Low</td><td>Reduces a common scene cost while preserving basic lighting readability.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cascaded shadows range / resolution</td><td>Low</td><td>Cuts distant shadow work during driving and dense outdoor views.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Volumetric fog resolution</td><td>Low</td><td>Preserves headroom in illuminated, effect-heavy scenes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Screen-space reflections quality</td><td>Low</td><td>Reduces expensive reflections in wet streets and reflective interiors.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Crowd density</td><td>Low</td><td>Limits additional CPU and scene pressure in populated city areas.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ray tracing</td><td>Off</td><td>Avoids a large rendering cost on integrated graphics and protects the frame-time budget.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Motion blur / film grain / chromatic aberration</td><td>Off for baseline test</td><td>Makes clarity and frame pacing easier to judge. Restore only according to visual preference.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">Menu names can change with game patches. Use the closest current option and save a screenshot before editing. Steam's minimum desktop-PC hardware is useful context, but it is not proof of a Legion Go result.</p>

    <h2>Tune Cyberpunk in a repeatable order</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Update and restart.</strong> Finish the stable Windows, Lenovo, graphics-driver, Legion Space, and game updates before diagnosing a repeatable problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Record the power condition.</strong> Note the Legion Go profile, charger state, display refresh rate, battery percentage, and background downloads.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply the baseline.</strong> Select 1280×800, 40 FPS, the Steam Deck preset, FSR Quality, Low crowd density, and ray tracing off.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the built-in benchmark.</strong> Save its average and minimum result for comparison; do not present one run as a universal device benchmark.</li>
      <li><strong>Drive a busy route.</strong> Test traffic, crowds, rapid camera movement, and a short combat encounter. Repeat the same route after every meaningful change.</li>
      <li><strong>Change one pressure point at a time.</strong> If 40 FPS is inconsistent, use FSR Balanced first, then lower reflections, fog, shadows, and crowd density before cutting textures.</li>
      <li><strong>Build a second profile only after the first passes.</strong> Compare 1200p/30 for extra clarity or 800p/60 for response, but do not mix those experiments into the baseline.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Three profiles worth comparing</h2>
    <div class="grid cols3">
      <section class="card">
        <span class="chip">Balanced baseline</span>
        <h3>800p / 40 FPS</h3>
        <p>Steam Deck preset, FSR Quality, Medium textures, Low crowd density, and ray tracing off. Move FSR to Balanced if the city-route test cannot hold the target acceptably.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="card">
        <span class="chip">Clarity experiment</span>
        <h3>1200p / 30 FPS</h3>
        <p>Keep the same power condition and Low expensive effects. Compare FSR Quality against the 800p profile and retain it only if text and distant detail improve enough to justify lower response.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="card">
        <span class="chip">Response experiment</span>
        <h3>800p / 60 FPS</h3>
        <p>Expect more aggressive compromises. Test FSR Balanced, lower effects, and a higher power profile separately. Reject this profile if busy traversal produces uneven pacing or distracting image softness.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <h2>Fix common Cyberpunk 2077 problems on Legion Go</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check first</th><th>Next step</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Benchmark passes but driving stutters</td><td>Repeat the same busy route with downloads paused</td><td>Lower crowd density, reflections, fog, and shadows; compare FSR Balanced</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Image looks too soft</td><td>Return to FSR Quality at 800p and disable dynamic scaling</td><td>Use modest sharpening, then compare 1200p/30 as a separate profile</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frame rate is unstable in crowds</td><td>Confirm Low crowd density and the same power mode</td><td>Use a 30 FPS cap for consistency before raising the power target</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hitching appears after an update</td><td>Restart, pause downloads, and verify free SSD space</td><td>Run the same benchmark and route twice before changing many settings</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Controller prompts are wrong</td><td>Set Legion Space controls to gamepad mode</td><td>Restore the default Steam Input or in-game controller layout and remove conflicting remaps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>New crashes or visual corruption</td><td>Restore the preset, ray tracing off, and remove unofficial config edits</td><td>Verify game files and check current CD PROJEKT RED and Lenovo support guidance</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Storage, battery, and dock planning</h2>
    <p>Steam currently lists <strong>70GB of available space</strong>, DirectX 12, 12GB minimum system memory, and an SSD requirement. Keep extra room for updates, shader data, saves, optional content, and Windows temporary files. Use the <a href="/calculators/storage-planner/">storage planner</a>, and check the <a href="/guides/handheld-pc-ssd-form-factor-guide/">handheld SSD form-factor guide</a> before buying an internal drive.</p>
    <p>This page does not invent a battery runtime. Read total system draw during your own repeatable route and enter it in the <a href="/calculators/battery-estimator/">battery estimator</a>. For plugged-in play, match the charger's USB-C PD capabilities to the device rather than buying on wattage text alone. For desk or TV play, use the <a href="/guides/handheld-gaming-usb-c-dock-checklist/">USB-C dock checklist</a>.</p>

    <h2>Optional gear after the settings are stable</h2>
    <p class="affiliate-note">Disclosure: these are broad Amazon searches marked sponsored/nofollow, not hands-on endorsements. Compare exact specifications, model fit, seller support, and return terms. No price, compatibility, performance, or battery claim is made; a real Associates tag is appended only when configured.</p>
    <div class="grid cols3">
      <a class="card gear-card" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=legion+go+65w+usb+c+pd+charger" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" data-affiliate-link data-category="chargers">
        <span class="chip">Sponsored search</span><h3>65W-class USB-C PD chargers</h3><p class="muted">Verify the single-port PD output, included cable rating, port-sharing behavior, warranty, and support for the exact Legion Go model.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card gear-card" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=legion+go+a2+u3+microsd+card" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" data-affiliate-link data-category="microsd-cards">
        <span class="chip">Sponsored search</span><h3>A2 / U3 microSD cards</h3><p class="muted">Compare capacity, seller traceability, warranty, and verification tools before moving a large game library.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card gear-card" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=legion+go+usb+c+dock+hdmi+65w" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" data-affiliate-link data-category="docks">
        <span class="chip">Sponsored search</span><h3>Legion Go USB-C docks</h3><p class="muted">Check HDMI mode support, USB-C PD passthrough, cable position, device clearance, Ethernet needs, and return terms.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p>Compare those categories in the <a href="/gear/">spec-based gear library</a> or plan the total setup with the <a href="/calculators/accessory-budget/">accessory budget calculator</a>.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="grid cols2">
      {faqs.map(faq => (
        <section class="card">
          <h3>{faq.question}</h3>
          <p>{faq.answer}</p>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2>Sources and methodology</h2>
    <p>Sources were checked on July 28, 2026. Steam supplies the current 70GB SSD requirement, 12GB minimum system-memory listing, DirectX 12 requirement, full-controller-support label, developer/publisher identity, and official image. Lenovo PSREF supplies the original Legion Go hardware context. CD PROJEKT RED supplies the official game site, and Lenovo Support supplies the device-support route. The profiles are conservative editorial starting sequences informed by those constraints, the game's built-in benchmark, and public configuration patterns. Handheld Settings Lab did not personally benchmark the current game, Windows, driver, or Legion Space build. Google autocomplete evidence saved in the July 2026 audit includes “Legion Go settings for Cyberpunk 2077”; that is query-language evidence, not search volume.</p>
    <ul>
      {sources.map(source => <li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">{source.label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>For the device-wide workflow, return to the <a href="/legion-go-settings/">Legion Go settings hub</a>. Use the <a href="/calculators/">battery, storage, and accessory calculators</a>, or browse the <a href="/gear/">gear library</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
